\DOC LOCAL_ADD_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ADD_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic {eventually} execution goal across an inlined
modular-addition subroutine, replacing the current state variable with a later
one and adding an assumption characterising the computed result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which code/offset configuration (i.e.
which calling context) to use; the second is a {string list} naming the
destination and the two operand buffers of the addition, which determine the
names used for the result variable and the memory locations read and written.
The conclusion is advanced to a fresh later state variable {s'}, with the
postcondition {P} unchanged:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s
   ================================================  LOCAL_ADD_P521_TAC n [dest; op1; op2]
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is obtained as follows. All assumptions of {A} mentioning
the old state (program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory
contents, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relation from the initial
state) are discharged and re-added with the new state variable, the program
counter advanced, and the {MAYCHANGE} frame extended with any further
components clobbered (additional registers and the destination buffer); purely
arithmetic side conditions are retained, sometimes being re-added rather than
kept in place. One new assumption records the destination buffer's contents in
the new state as a variable, and another gives that variable's value as the
modular sum of the two operands under hypotheses that each operand is below the
modulus; the result variable is renamed with a prime if the given name already
occurs. Assumptions not referring to any state (such as non-overlap conditions)
are left untouched.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of this symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
